  "query_preview": "..Recent satellite imagery shows smoke from fires\nacross the Pacific Northwest lingering across central and northern\nUtah and southwest Wyoming this afternoon. Westerly flow\ndeveloping on the northern fringe of a strengthening high \npressure ridge is expected to scour some of this smoke out of \nnorthern Utah overnight, but some haze may linger into Thursday. \nAcross central Utah, the Widemouth 2 Fire continues to output high\nconcentrations of near surface smoke. This fire will continue to \nimpact surrounding areas while it remains active, but more \nefficient mixing on Thursday will keep the impact footprint \nsmaller as smoke becomes more vertically integrated into the \natmosphere.\n\nThe aforementioned ridge will continue to amplify over the Great\nBasin through Saturday, resulting in temperatures climbing well\nabove average through this period. High temperatures peak on\nSaturday, with highs around 10 degrees above average. Low\ntemperatures gradually increase through the weekend as well, with\nthe Wasatch Front and lower Washington County seeing exacerbated\nheat impacts as a result. Given this, Extreme Heat Warnings were \nissued for most southern Utah valleys Thursday through Saturday, \nand the Wasatch Front, Great Salt Lake Desert, and Castle\nCountry/San Rafael Swell through Sunday. Heat Advisories are in\neffect for all other valleys across the state. \n\nIntense heating under this anomalous ridge will result in\nincreased chances for afternoon showers and thunderstorms, mainly\nacross southwest Utah, beginning Thursday. Chances for afternoon\nconvective development will continue through the weekend, with the\nmain impact from storms through Saturday being dry lightning and \ngusty and erratic outflow winds, given dry lower levels through\nthis period. The ridge shifts eastward Sunday, introducing a more\nfavorable southerly flow pattern for monsoon moisture to spread\ninto the region through early next week. Storms will transition\nwetter by Sunday across southwest Utah, with coverage expanding\nnorthward into early next week as moisture continues to spread\nnorth. The potential for wetter storms on Sunday raises concerns\nfor locally heavy rainfall to produce flash flooding, especially\nfor slot canyons, recent burn scars, and normally dry washes.",
